  "from_addr": "Mike Allen <mike@axios.com>",
  "subject": "🤏 Axios Finish Line: Tiny things trending",
  "date": "Tue, 06 Oct 2026 21:29:24 -0400",
  "body_text": "96 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n 🌊 Plus: Pacific pause | Tuesday, October 06, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n View in browser \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n Axios Finish Line \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n By Mike Allen and Erica Pandey and Jim VandeHei &middot; Oct 06, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n 😎 Hello, Tuesday! Smart Brevity™ count: 494 words … 2 mins. Edited by Amy Stern. \n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 1 big thing: Tiny hobby boom \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n A miniature painter's desk, using a penny for scale, on display after the 2026 session. Photo: David Bearden/IGMA Guild School At miniature school, the grapes are the size of poppy seeds — and making even a handful can take hours, Axios' Natalie Daher writes. \n\n 🖼️ The big picture: Miniatures are having an unlikely revival, drawing younger people to an exacting, expensive and intensely analog hobby that demands hours of uninterrupted attention.\n\n Zoom in: Bloomberg News reporter Madison Darbyshire went to Guild School , the International Guild of Miniature Artisans' annual gathering in Castine, Maine — otherwise known as \"minitopia,\" or summer camp for miniaturists. She found:\n\n 🪑 A speck of sawdust can ruin the balance of a tiny Hans Wegner chair. 🧶 Micro-crocheting hooks can be as thin as sewing needles. 🧀 A cheese board \"the width of a silver dollar\" comes with polymer-set of clay Parmesan rounds that actually crumble, Darbyshire writes. A series of miniature sofas designed and created by the graduating class of 2026. Photo: Audrey Tripp/IGMA Guild School The standard scale is 1:12. That's 1,728 times smaller by volume than real life. \n\n 🍇 When Darbyshire's first set of grapes came out too big, her instructor said they were better suited to Barbie — whose 1:6 scale is \"looked down on in the miniatures community,\" Darbyshire writes . The pieces are tiny, but the price tags aren't. Most students paid $1,200-$1,550 in tuition, plus meals and travel costs. One miniature tea table sold at the school's auction for $7,600 (!). \n\n One longtime collector estimated that her collection is worth $250,000. Another miniaturist joked that there are months when she's weighing buying groceries against building a tiny haberdashery cabinet: \"It's the most fiscally irresponsible thing I've ever done as an adult.\" Board Member Peggy Tyler is seen here assembling a miniature. Photo: David Bearden/IGMA Guild School 📈 And the tiny world is getting bigger: This year's Guild School drew 232 people, including a record 82 first-timers. Preregistration for 2027 filled in four hours. \n\n How we got here: In the late 2010s, the hobby seemed headed for extinction as its practitioners aged and its traditional Colonial-and-Victorian aesthetic struggled to win over newcomers.\n\n Then came the pandemic, 3D printing, Instagram and TikTok. Suddenly younger makers with \"pink hair and tattoos\" were photographing everything and embracing '70s furniture, cottagecore and other styles older miniaturists had resisted. 🧠 The deeper appeal: The work is almost absurdly demanding — and that's part of the draw.\n\n Miniaturists told Bloomberg the hobby slows down a fast world, creates a flow state and gives them a contained universe they can control. Some re-create homes or places they've lost; others build houses they could never afford at full size. 😭 By midweek, Guild School has a name for the moment when students realize how much work is left: Weeping Wednesday.\n\n Rumor has it one student broke down in miniature potholder class. Then it was back to work. \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\nA MESSAGE FROM AXIOS\r\n\n \r\n See what's next for media in 2027 \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n Media Trends Executive Annual members receive the 2026 Annual Report, our special media document featuring global forecasts, sector analysis and exclusive media data insights.\r\n\n\r\n \r\n🔒 Get the report by becoming a Media Trends Executive member.\r\n\n\r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 2. 🌴 California dreaming \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Photo: Natalie Daher I snapped this view of the Pacific and Santa Monica Mountains on a cloudless morning yesterday from Santa Monica, Calif.\n\n I'm on the ground producing an upcoming Axios Show interview with longevity entrepreneur Bryan Johnson. 🎥 Subscribe to our YouTube , and keep an eye out for our episode later this week.\n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n 🏁 Please invite your friends to join Finish Line . \n\n \r\n 👆 Like this comms style and format? It's called Smart Brevity®. Bring it to your org — via hands-on training or internal comms software — to harness its power and impact. \r\n &nbsp; Axios thanks our partners for supporting our journalism.\n Advertise with us. \n\n Read more about Axios' mission , ethics and how we use AI in our journalism . You can reach the authors by replying to this email.\nSponsorship has no influence on editorial content.\n\n Thank you for signing up for this Axios newsletter. To stop receiving this newsletter, unsubscribe or manage your email preferences . If someone forwarded this to you, sign up now to get Axios in your inbox.\n\n Axios, PO Box 101060, Arlington VA 22201 &nbsp; Follow Axios across:\n\n &nbsp; &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;",
