">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td style=\"padding:20px 3.5% 10px;line-height:1.5\">\r\n<span class=\"b k\" style=\"font-size:27px;font-family:'gorditamedium',Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;color:#222;letter-spacing:-0.1px;line-height:1.5\">  1 big thing: Tiny hobby boom </span>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>   <tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr> <tr>\r\n<td class=\"v\" style=\"padding:0 3.5%;line-height:1.75;font-size:17px;font-family:Georgia,serif;color:#222;letter-spacing:-0.1px\"><div align=\"center\"><!--[if mso]><img alt='' src='https://images.axios.com/bdiEkTRIISMnn8E43h9mUZZ6xjg=/fit-in/1116x1300/2026/10/05/1791225242370.jpeg' style='display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;' width='558' /><![endif]--><!--[if !mso]><!--><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://images.axios.com/bdiEkTRIISMnn8E43h9mUZZ6xjg=/fit-in/1116x1300/2026/10/05/1791225242370.jpeg\" style=\"display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;\" width=\"100%\"/><!--<![endif]--></div> <div class=\"bodytext\" style=\"font-size:13px; font-family:'Gordita',Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif; color:#848484; margin-bottom:20px;\">A miniature painter's desk, using a penny for scale, on display after the 2026 session. Photo: David Bearden/IGMA Guild School</div><p><strong>At miniature school, </strong>the grapes are the size of poppy seeds — and making even a handful can take hours, <em>Axios' Natalie Daher writes.</em></p><p><strong>🖼️ The big picture: </strong>Miniatures are having an unlikely revival, drawing younger people to an exacting, expensive and intensely analog hobby that demands hours of uninterrupted attention.</p><p><strong>Zoom in: </strong>Bloomberg News reporter Madison Darbyshire <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47816884.\" target=\"_blank\">went to Guild School</a>, the International Guild of Miniature Artisans' annual gathering in Castine, Maine — otherwise known as \"minitopia,\" or summer camp for miniaturists. She found:</p><ul><li>🪑<strong>A speck of sawdust</strong> can ruin the balance of a tiny Hans Wegner chair. </li><li>🧶 <strong>Micro-crocheting</strong> <strong>hooks</strong> can be as thin as sewing needles. </li><li>🧀 <strong>A cheese board</strong> \"the width of a silver dollar\" comes with polymer-set of clay Parmesan rounds that actually crumble, Darbyshire writes. </li></ul><div align=\"center\"><!--[if mso]><img alt='' src='https://images.axios.com/aZIdyJ4gD3grMzYOCS5ItFrBIHU=/fit-in/1116x1300/2026/10/05/1791225307904.jpeg' style='display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;' width='558' /><![endif]--><!--[if !mso]><!--><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://images.axios.com/aZIdyJ4gD3grMzYOCS5ItFrBIHU=/fit-in/1116x1300/2026/10/05/1791225307904.jpeg\" style=\"display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;\" width=\"100%\"/><!--<![endif]--></div> <div class=\"bodytext\" style=\"font-size:13px; font-family:'Gordita',Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif; color:#848484; margin-bottom:20px;\">A series of miniature sofas designed and created by the graduating class of 2026. Photo: Audrey Tripp/IGMA Guild School</div><p><strong>The standard scale</strong> is 1:12. That's 1,728 times smaller by volume than real life. </p><ul><li>🍇<strong>When Darbyshire's first set of grapes</strong> came out too big, her instructor said they were better suited to Barbie — whose 1:6 scale is \"looked down on in the miniatures community,\" <em>Darbyshire writes</em>.</li></ul><p><strong>The pieces are tiny, </strong>but the price tags aren't. Most students paid $1,200-$1,550 in tuition, plus meals and travel costs. One miniature tea table sold at the school's auction for $7,600 (!). </p><ul><li><strong>One longtime collector</strong> estimated that her collection is worth $250,000.</li><li><strong>Another miniaturist</strong> joked that there are months when she's weighing buying groceries against building a tiny haberdashery cabinet: \"It's the most fiscally irresponsible thing I've ever done as an adult.\"</li></ul><div align=\"center\"><!--[if mso]><img alt='' src='https://images.axios.com/V9dMensz85Y_b752rzubEfs7SnA=/fit-in/1116x1300/2026/10/05/1791225590560.jpeg' style='display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;' width='558' /><![endif]--><!--[if !mso]><!--><img alt=\"\" src=\"https://images.axios.com/V9dMensz85Y_b752rzubEfs7SnA=/fit-in/1116x1300/2026/10/05/1791225590560.jpeg\" style=\"display:block; margin: 0 auto; max-width:100%;\" width=\"100%\"/><!--<![endif]--></div> <div class=\"bodytext\" style=\"font-size:13px; font-family:'Gordita',Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif; color:#848484; margin-bottom:20px;\">Board<strong> </strong>Member Peggy Tyler is seen here assembling a miniature. Photo: David Bearden/IGMA Guild School</div><p>📈<strong>And the tiny world is getting bigger:</strong> This year's Guild School drew 232 people, including a record 82 first-timers. Preregistration for 2027 filled in four hours. </p><p><strong>How we got here:</strong> In the late 2010s, the hobby seemed headed for extinction as its practitioners aged and its traditional Colonial-and-Victorian aesthetic struggled to win over newcomers.</p><ul><li><strong>Then came</strong> the pandemic, 3D printing, Instagram and TikTok.</li><li><strong>Suddenly younger makers</strong> with \"pink hair and tattoos\" were photographing everything and embracing '70s furniture, cottagecore and other styles older miniaturists had resisted.</li></ul><p>🧠 <strong>The deeper appeal:</strong> The work is almost absurdly demanding — and that's part of the draw.</p><ul><li><strong>Miniaturists told Bloomberg</strong> the hobby slows down a fast world, creates a flow state and gives them a contained universe they can control. </li><li><strong>Some re-create homes</strong> or places they've lost; others build houses they could never afford at full size.</li></ul><p>😭 <strong>By midweek,</strong> Guild School has a name for the moment when students realize how much work is left: Weeping Wednesday.</p><ul><li><strong>Rumor has it</strong> one student broke down in miniature potholder class. Then it was back to work.</li></ul></td>\r\n</tr> <tr><td align=\"center\" style=\"padding:20px 3.5% 25px\">   <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47816884.402761/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuZmFjZWJvb2suY29tL3NoYXJlci5waHA_dT1odHRwczovL3d3dy5heGlvcy5jb20vbmV3c2xldHRlcnMvYXhpb3MtZmluaXNoLWxpbmUtZmNkMjYxZDUtZjNmMC00MDcwLWFhNWEtNWFmYmQ5Y2E5ZTYyLmh0bWw_Y2h1bmslM0QxJTI2dXRtX3Rlcm0lM0RmYnNvY2lhbHNoYXJlJTIzc3RvcnkxJnV0bV9zb3VyY2U9bmV3c2xldHRlciZ1dG1fbWVkaXVtPWVtYWlsJnV0bV9jYW1wYWlnbj1uZXdzbGV0dGVyX2F4aW9zZmluaXNobGluZSZzdHJlYW09dG9w/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB33dda671\" style=\"display:inline-block\"><img width=\"32\" height=\"32\" style=\"border:0\" src=\"https://static.axios.com/img/axios-site/newsletters/logos/footer/share-facebook-circle-border.png\" alt=\"Share on Facebook\"></a> <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47816884.402761/aHR0cHM6Ly90d2l0dGVyLmNvbS9pbnRlbnQvdHdlZXQ_\" style=\"display:inline-block;margin-left:7px\"><img width=\"32\" height=\"32\" style=\"border:0\" src=\"https://static.axios.com/img/axios-site/newsletters/logos/footer/share-x-circle-border.png\" alt=\"Tweet this Story\"></a> <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47816884.\" style=\"display:inline-block;margin-left:7px\"><img width=\"32\" height=\"32\" style=\"border:0\" src=\"https://static.axios.com/img/axios-site/newsletters/logos/footer/share-linkedin-circle-border.png\" alt=\"Post to LinkedIn\"></a>  <a href=\"mailto:?subject=From Axios: 1 big thing: Tiny hobby boom&body=teruyuki%40hh.iij4u.or.jp%20has%20shared%20an%20Axios%20story%20with%20you%3A%0A%0A1 big thing: Tiny hobby boom%0Ahttps%3A%2F%2Fwww.axios.com%2Fnewsletters%2Faxios-finish-line-fcd261d5-f3f0-4070-aa5a-5afbd9ca9e62.html%3Fchunk%3D1%26utm_term%3Demshare%23story1\" style=\"display:inline-block;margin-left:7px\"><img width=\"32\" height=\"32\" style=\"border:0\" src=\"https://static.axios.com/img/axios-site/newsletters/logos/footer/share-email-circle-border.png\" alt=\"Email this Story\"></a> <a href